;;; Effect.mon --- the standard effect basis

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.3.0
:keywords effects operations resumptions capabilities

;;; Commentary:

 This module names effects; the compiler only elaborates their calculus.
 A declaration reads:

   (effect Name Trait Operation Resumes Scoped [Input Output])

 =Resumes= is a quantity: 0 means abortive, 1 means one-shot, and omega
 means unrestricted. =Scoped= says whether the operation introduces a
 capability whose identity must not escape. Input and Output describe the
 operation itself, not the type of a handled computation.

 The typing judgment used by the elaborator is

   Sigma ; Psi | Gamma |- e : A ! epsilon |> Delta

 Read it as: under global signatures Sigma, modal context Psi, and
 quantitative resources Gamma, expression e returns A, performs epsilon,
 and leaves resource demand Delta. A function arrow =A -e-> B= stores
 epsilon latently until application.

 Effect rows obey join and handling:

   effects(e1 ; e2)       = effects(e1) join effects(e2)
   handle <op | epsilon>  = epsilon
   continuation demand    = Resumes * continuation use

 Traits form a Core-declared preorder below. For example, allocation implies
 state, and state implies io. No effect names are built into the compiler.

 Operation identity, trait inclusion, resumption multiplicity, and capability
 scoping are independent axes. Sharing a trait does not make operations
 interchangeable, and an unrestricted resumption is never inferred merely
 because its trait is =control=. The declarations below are therefore the
 normative standard basis, not a closed compiler enumeration.

;;;; =Higher-Order Effect Boundary=

 The compact source surface currently records a latent effect on an ordinary
 function arrow, but it cannot yet quantify over the latent row of a callback.
 A library signature such as =(() -> a)= must therefore be read as a genuinely
 pure callback; Core must not document such a value as though it were silently
 effect-polymorphic. Owned-resource combinators use pure callbacks until the
 language has a source-level higher-order row abstraction.

 The eventual higher-order contract must preserve four facts simultaneously:
 the callback's row, effects performed by the owner itself, resumption quantity,
 and scoped capability identity/quantity. In particular, abstracting over a row
 must never make a scoped or quantity-1 capability escapable or duplicable. This
 is a missing *expression surface*, not permission for individual libraries to
 invent incompatible callback-effect conventions.

;;; Code:

module Effect []

| Runtime interaction
(effect Core.IO.read io read 1 False Unit String)
(effect Core.IO.write io write 1 False String Unit)
(effect Core.Trace.emit telemetry emit 1 False String Unit)

| Memory and foreign execution
(effect Core.Foreign.asm foreign asm 1 False)
(effect Core.Alloc.heap allocate heap 1 False)
(effect Core.State.write state write 1 False)

| Control effects
(effect Core.Exception.raise exception raise 0 False String a)
(effect Core.Control.diverge diverge diverge 0 False)
(effect Core.Control.yield control yield omega False)

| Scoped capabilities
(effect Core.Scope.local state local 1 True)

| Named handler policies. These select continuation usage; lowering remains a
| generic compiler decision justified by the resulting proof.
effect-handler Core.Exception.abort Core.Exception.raise 0 True
effect-handler Core.IO.read-once Core.IO.read 1 True
effect-handler Core.Control.yield-once Core.Control.yield 1 True
effect-handler Core.Scope.local-once Core.Scope.local 1 True

| Trait preorder
(effect-trait allocate state)
(effect-trait state io)
(effect-trait foreign io)
